        <div id="who-should-buy" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">7. Who Should Buy a RHYTHM FUN Treadmill</h2>
<p>
            <strong>A RHYTHM FUN is a reasonable buy</strong> if you want a compact incline walking pad with
            handlebars for under $210, you weigh well under 300 lb, and you will mostly walk. The 13% pad is
            the one whose figures we could check, and the one we would choose.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Look elsewhere</strong> if you are tall, heavy or want to run, since every RHYTHM FUN has a
            belt of about 37.5 inches; if you want a motorised incline; or if you want more than a year of
            warranty. Our <a href="/best-walking-pads-with-incline/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best walking pads with incline</a>
            and <a href="/best-walking-pads-with-handrail/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best walking pads with handrails</a>
            roundups cover both types, and our
            <a href="/best-folding-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best folding treadmills</a>
            roundup covers machines with longer decks.
          </p>
          <p>
            Whichever you choose, set it up so it lasts. Put the pad on a firm, level surface or a
            <a href="/do-you-need-a-treadmill-mat/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>treadmill mat</a>, not deep carpet, so the motor housing can breathe and the deck does not flex.
            Check the belt is centred and not slipping after the first few hours, when new belts stretch,
            and lubricate it on the schedule in the manual. Fold the handlebars down and lift the incline
            foot only with the belt stopped. Our guide to
            <a href="/how-to-make-a-cheap-treadmill-last-longer/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">making a cheap treadmill last longer</a>
            covers the rest, and most of it costs nothing.
